{
  "skillId": "recursion",
  "title": "Recursion: a method that calls itself",
  "minutes": 5,
  "version": 1,
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "hook",
      "type": "hook",
      "title": "Solve it by solving a smaller one",
      "body": "Factorials, tree traversals, merge sort: some problems are easiest to describe **in terms of themselves**. A recursive method does exactly that, and Java keeps every pending call on its **call stack**.\n\nIn five minutes you’ll watch the stack grow and shrink, one call at a time, then write a recursive line yourself."
    },
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this program print?",
      "code": "public class Main {\n    static int fact(int n) {\n        if (n <= 1) return 1; //~ The base case: stop here. Without it, fact would call itself forever.\n        return n * fact(n - 1); //~ n × the factorial of n − 1. Watch a new call pile onto the stack.\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(fact(4)); //~ Start with fact(4). It can't answer until fact(3) does…\n    }\n}",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "4",
          "why": "fact(4) waits for fact(3), which waits for fact(2), and so on, and then the results multiply on the way back."
        },
        {
          "text": "24",
          "why": "Right: 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 24, worked out as the calls return."
        },
        {
          "text": "10",
          "why": "That’s 4 + 3 + 2 + 1. The method multiplies."
        },
        {
          "text": "Crashes: StackOverflowError",
          "why": "The base case (n <= 1) stops the calls at n = 1, so the stack never overflows."
        }
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
