    {
      "id": "ideas",
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "How recursion works",
      "points": [
        "A recursive method **calls itself** on a smaller input.",
        "It needs a **base case** that answers directly. Without one: `StackOverflowError`.",
        "Every call gets its **own** copy of its variables (its own stack frame).",
        "Answers come back **in reverse order**, from the deepest call up to the first."
      ],
      "pattern": "static int f(int n) {\n    if (n == 0) return 0;      // base case\n    return n + f(n - 1);       // smaller problem\n}"
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "A classic trap. What does this print?",
      "code": "public class Main {\n    static void countdown(int n) {\n        if (n == 0) return;\n        countdown(n - 1);\n        System.out.print(n + \" \");\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        countdown(3);\n    }\n}",
      "options": [
        {
          "text": "3 2 1 0",
          "why": "n == 0 returns before anything is printed."
        },
        {
          "text": "3 2 1",
          "why": "The print comes AFTER the recursive call, so it runs as the calls return, deepest (1) first."
        },
        {
          "text": "0 1 2 3",
          "why": "The n == 0 call returns immediately without printing."
        },
        {
          "text": "1 2 3",
          "why": "Yes. countdown(3) calls countdown(2) before printing, and so on down to 1, which prints first."
        }
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "fill",
      "type": "fill",
      "prompt": "Your turn. Make sum(n) return 1 + 2 + … + n using recursion.",
      "code": "public class Main {\n    static int sum(int n) {\n        if (n == 0) return 0;\n        return ____;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(sum(4));\n        System.out.println(sum(1));\n        System.out.println(sum(0));\n    }\n}",
      "accepted": [
        "n + sum(n - 1)",
        "sum(n - 1) + n",
        "n + sum(n-1)"
      ],
      "wrong": [
        "n + sum(n)",
        "sum(n - 1)",
        "n"
      ],
      "hint": "The sum up to n is n plus the sum up to n − 1.",
      "explain": "`n + sum(n - 1)` shrinks the problem by one each call until the base case returns 0.",
      "expectedOutput": "10\n1\n0"
    }
